#: Readme_CUDA.md:1
msgid "EXTRA: GPU offloading with Cuda"
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:2
msgid ""
"To get the cuda code compiled I set up an ad hoc alternative to `plumed "
"mklib`, that uses nvcc to compile the kernel and to link the shared object. "
"The example is extremely simplified and should not be used in the real world"
" as is, because it contains a collection of **bad ideas**."
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:4
msgid "The calculate insterface is simple:"
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:12
msgid ""
"it calls `getCoordination(pos, R_0)`, that is an interface to the kernel:"
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:36
msgid ""
"there the data is initialized and loaded on the GPU with a combination of "
"`cudaMalloc` and `cudaMemcpy` and then the two kernels are called with the "
"`<<<NBlocks,threadPerBlock>>>` syntax."
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:38
msgid "Citing the Nvidia blog:"
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:39
msgid ""
"A group of threads is called a CUDA block. CUDA blocks are grouped into a "
"grid. A kernel is executed as a grid of blocks of threads."
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:41
msgid "Each thread execute the instructions in the kernel."
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:43
msgid ""
"In this case we have a kernel for calculation the `ncoord` value of each "
"atom:"
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:66
msgid ""
"You have to imagine that the kernel is more or less the body of the outer "
"for loop of the previous examples and to get the outer index you have to "
"check in which thread you are: `threadIdx` and `blockIdx` that contains the "
"information about the index of the thread or of the block. In this case the "
"kernel is called as a block of `nat` threads (and that is a very **bad "
"idea**, since `nat` may be not a power of 2 or be bigger than the maximum "
"number of thread avaiable in the gpu)."
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:69
msgid "EXTRA REMARKS"
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:71
msgid ""
"Also the kernel is made in order that can be called with `<<<1, nat>>>` or "
"`<<<nat, 1>>>` without any error, due to the index bein calculated always as"
" a sum `const int i = threadIdx.x + blockIdx.x;` that is `0+something` or "
"`something+0`if the threads or the blocks are 1. This is a **bad idea**."
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:74
msgid ""
"Then the coordination must be summed (in a very simple case like this it may"
" be faster doing the process in for loop on the cpu) with a reduction "
"algorithm:"
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:91
msgid "`blockDim` contains the number of thread in the block"
msgstr ""

#: Readme_CUDA.md:93
msgid ""
"The sum is made in pair to each element of the passed array and stored in "
"one of the two used indexes of the array. And the operation is repeaded on "
"half of the array, and so on until we are reduced to one element that is the"
" result."
msgstr ""

